std.net — Wire-format packet helpers
=====================================

Low-level byte-buffer helpers and protocol constants for building
and parsing link-layer / network-layer packets: Ethernet II, ARP,
IPv4, ICMP. Paired with the core `std/net/net.lsysl` (IPv4, MAC,
byte-order); both files belong to `module std.net` so callers see
a single flat import surface.

Everything here is pure — no OS dependencies — so kernel drivers
(e.g. `oskit/drivers/virtio/net_handler.lsysl`) and the inet
server can both import the same helpers.

    module std.net

    import std.debug.{assert}


Ethernet II
-----------

Common EtherType values. The 16-bit field sits at offset 12 of
every Ethernet II frame, big-endian.

    val ETHERTYPE_IPV4 = 0x0800
    val ETHERTYPE_ARP  = 0x0806
    val ETHERTYPE_IPV6 = 0x86dd


ARP
---

Opcodes for the 2-byte `oper` field at offset 6 of the ARP
message (offset 20 of the encapsulating Ethernet frame).

    val ARP_OP_REQUEST = 1
    val ARP_OP_REPLY   = 2


IPv4
----

`IP_PROTO_*` mirrors POSIX's `IPPROTO_*` (`std.net.net`) for use at
the byte-field level. Keeping both names avoids churn in existing
call sites while letting packet code read `IP_PROTO_ICMP` at the
offset-9 protocol field.

    val IP_PROTO_ICMP = 1
    val IP_PROTO_TCP  = 6
    val IP_PROTO_UDP  = 17


ICMP
----

Type codes for the 1-byte `type` field at the start of the ICMP
message. We only handle echo request / echo reply today.

    val ICMP_ECHO_REQ = 8
    val ICMP_ECHO_REP = 0


Byte-buffer helpers
-------------------

All wire-format multi-byte fields are big-endian. These helpers
read/write 16-bit fields at arbitrary byte offsets without any
alignment assumptions — pointers come from IPC buffers or NIC
DMA regions.


### pkt_read_u16

Read a big-endian u16 from `p + off`. Returns an `int` so callers
can compare against plain integer literals.

    pkt_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)


### pkt_write_u16

Write `v` as a big-endian u16 into `p + off`. Only the low 16 bits
of `v` are used.

    pkt_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)


### pkt_write_mac

Copy 6 bytes from `mac` into `p + off`. No byte swapping — MAC
addresses are already on-the-wire order in both memory and
packets.

    pkt_write_mac(p: *byte, off: int, mac: *byte)
        var i = 0
        while i < 6
            p[off + i] = mac[i]
            i += 1


### pkt_write_ipv4

Write an IPv4 address as 4 consecutive bytes (`a.b.c.d` order, MSB
first) into `p + off`. Takes `int` octets rather than a `u32` so
callers can emit literal addresses without constructing an IP
value first.

    pkt_write_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int, a: int, b: int, c: int, d: int)
        p[off]     = byte(a & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(b & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte(c & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte(d & 0xff)


### pkt_match_ipv4

Compare the 4-byte IPv4 field at `p + off` against the octets
`a.b.c.d`. Returns 1 on match, 0 otherwise. Used by packet
handlers to check "is this destined for us" without allocating.

    pkt_match_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int, a: int, b: int, c: int, d: int) -> int
        if int(p[off])     != (a & 0xff)
            return 0
        if int(p[off + 1]) != (b & 0xff)
            return 0
        if int(p[off + 2]) != (c & 0xff)
            return 0
        if int(p[off + 3]) != (d & 0xff)
            return 0
        1


Internet checksum (RFC 1071)
----------------------------

16-bit one's-complement sum over `len` bytes at `p`. Used by IPv4
headers and ICMP messages. The caller is responsible for zeroing
the checksum field in the buffer before calling (the checksum is
computed over the full buffer including that field).

    net_checksum(p: *byte, len: int) -> int
        var sum = 0
        var i = 0
        while i + 1 < len
            sum += pkt_read_u16(p, i)
            i += 2
        if i < len
            sum += (int(p[i]) & 0xff) << 8
        while (sum >> 16) != 0
            sum = (sum & 0xffff) + (sum >> 16)
        (~sum) & 0xffff


Tests
=====

### u16 round-trip

    #test
    test_pkt_u16_roundtrip() -> unit
        var buf: [4]byte
        pkt_write_u16(buf, 0, 0x1234)
        pkt_write_u16(buf, 2, 0xbeef)
        assert(buf[0] == 0x12, "hi byte at 0")
        assert(buf[1] == 0x34, "lo byte at 1")
        assert(pkt_read_u16(buf, 0) == 0x1234, "read back offset 0")
        assert(pkt_read_u16(buf, 2) == 0xbeef, "read back offset 2")


### u16 write truncates

High bits above 16 are masked off.

    #test
    test_pkt_u16_truncates_high_bits() -> unit
        var buf: [2]byte
        pkt_write_u16(buf, 0, 0xdead1234)
        assert(buf[0] == 0x12, "hi byte truncated")
        assert(buf[1] == 0x34, "lo byte")


### MAC copy

    #test
    test_pkt_write_mac_copies_six_bytes() -> unit
        var src: [6]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 6
            src[i] = byte(0xa0 + i)
            i += 1
        var buf: [8]byte
        buf[0] = 0x11
        buf[7] = 0x22
        pkt_write_mac(buf, 1, src)
        assert(buf[0] == 0x11, "before untouched")
        assert(buf[1] == 0xa0, "mac[0]")
        assert(buf[2] == 0xa1, "mac[1]")
        assert(buf[6] == 0xa5, "mac[5]")
        assert(buf[7] == 0x22, "after untouched")


### IPv4 write

    #test
    test_pkt_write_ipv4_order_abcd() -> unit
        var buf: [4]byte
        pkt_write_ipv4(buf, 0, 192, 168, 1, 1)
        assert(buf[0] == 192, "a")
        assert(buf[1] == 168, "b")
        assert(buf[2] == 1, "c")
        assert(buf[3] == 1, "d")


### IPv4 match

    #test
    test_pkt_match_ipv4_matches_and_rejects() -> unit
        var buf: [4]byte
        pkt_write_ipv4(buf, 0, 10, 0, 2, 15)
        assert(pkt_match_ipv4(buf, 0, 10, 0, 2, 15) == 1, "matches self")
        assert(pkt_match_ipv4(buf, 0, 10, 0, 2, 14) == 0, "wrong last octet")
        assert(pkt_match_ipv4(buf, 0, 10, 0, 2, 16) == 0, "other last octet")
        assert(pkt_match_ipv4(buf, 0, 192, 168, 1, 1) == 0, "entirely different")


### Checksum — empty and all-zero

    #test
    test_net_checksum_zero_bytes() -> unit
        var buf: [4]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 4
            buf[i] = 0
            i += 1
        assert(net_checksum(buf, 4) == 0xffff, "zero bytes checksum is 0xffff")


### Checksum — known IPv4 header

RFC 1071's worked example uses the IPv4 header `45 00 00 73 00 00
40 00 40 11 00 00 c0 a8 00 01 c0 a8 00 c7`, which has checksum
`0xb861`. The checksum field (bytes 10..11) is zero when the sum
is computed.

    #test
    test_net_checksum_rfc1071_example() -> unit
        var buf: [20]byte
        buf[0]  = 0x45
        buf[1]  = 0x00
        buf[2]  = 0x00
        buf[3]  = 0x73
        buf[4]  = 0x00
        buf[5]  = 0x00
        buf[6]  = 0x40
        buf[7]  = 0x00
        buf[8]  = 0x40
        buf[9]  = 0x11
        buf[10] = 0x00
        buf[11] = 0x00
        buf[12] = 0xc0
        buf[13] = 0xa8
        buf[14] = 0x00
        buf[15] = 0x01
        buf[16] = 0xc0
        buf[17] = 0xa8
        buf[18] = 0x00
        buf[19] = 0xc7
        assert(net_checksum(buf, 20) == 0xb861, "RFC 1071 IPv4 header checksum")


### Checksum — odd length

Odd-length buffers pad the final byte into the high half of a u16.

    #test
    test_net_checksum_odd_length() -> unit
        var buf: [3]byte
        buf[0] = 0x12
        buf[1] = 0x34
        buf[2] = 0x56
        // sum = 0x1234 + 0x5600 = 0x6834 → ~ = 0x97cb
        assert(net_checksum(buf, 3) == 0x97cb, "odd length padding")


### Checksum — verification round-trip

A buffer with its checksum written back in should checksum to 0.

    #test
    test_net_checksum_roundtrip_to_zero() -> unit
        var buf: [6]byte
        buf[0] = 0x11
        buf[1] = 0x22
        buf[2] = 0x33
        buf[3] = 0x44
        buf[4] = 0x00
        buf[5] = 0x00
        val c = net_checksum(buf, 6)
        pkt_write_u16(buf, 4, c)
        assert(net_checksum(buf, 6) == 0, "verify sums to 0")
